      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Proponents of segregation argued that certain groups were inherently suited for specific types of labor or social roles based on their perceived racial characteristics. For example, they claimed that non-white populations were naturally inclined towards manual labor, such as farming, construction, and domestic work, due to their supposed physical strength and endurance. They argued that white people, on the other hand, were better suited for intellectual and professional pursuits, such as education, business, and government, due to their supposed superior cognitive abilities and moral character.\n\nThey justified this viewpoint by citing the work of eugenicists such as Madison Grant, who argued that different races had distinct biological and psychological characteristics that determined their aptitudes for different types of work. They also pointed to the supposed historical and cultural evidence of different racial groups performing different types of labor, such as the supposed African American tradition of farming and the supposed European American tradition of intellectual pursuits.\n\nIn terms of presenting this argument in a debate, they might argue that segregation was necessary to allow each group to fulfill its \"natural\" role in society, and that this was not about malice or prejudice, but about creating an efficient and harmonious social order. They might claim that by separating the races, they were able to eliminate conflicts and competition",
